Phloem-Riot
Ingredients: JAZZ RAP / NATIVE TONGUE + FUTURE BASS & FUTURE GARAGE + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Bagpipes  
Creative Catalyst: its founding artists were botanists who sonified the electrical signals of rainforest plants.

Phloem-Riot is an eco-insurrectionist sonic collision where the dense, polysyllabic lyrical dexterity of Native Tongue jazz rap is violently transplanted into the stuttering, 2-step syncopation of UK future garage and the concussive, neon-free weight of future bass. Rejecting pastoral folk clichés and jazz rap chillness, the genre turns Highland bagpipes into screaming pneumatic reed weapons, routing their piercing drones through modular synths clocked to the live micro-voltage spikes of traumatized jungle flora.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 134–138 BPM. A loose, unquantized future garage shuffle laden with swung ghost-snares, suddenly ambushed by half-time, gut-slamming future bass drops and jagged, rapid-fire boom-bap syllable pacing.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark modal jazz harmonies (Phrygian-dominant, minor 11ths, flat-5 suspensions) clashing violently with the untamed, piercing 480 Hz modal drone of the Great Highland bagpipe chanter.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Galvanic Transpiration Sidechaining (an impossible biocellular routing where sub-bass decay and chord filters are modulated live by real-time galvanic resistance shifts of wounded broadleaf plants).
  2. Chanter Granular Smearing (bagpipe drones sliced into hyper-dense granular clouds that mimic the buzzing drone of cloud-forest hornets).
  3. Lo-Fi Microphonic Foley (drum kits layered with high-gain hydrophone recordings of xylem cavitation, snapping twigs, and hydraulic plant pumps).
- Dynamics: Brooding, atmospheric quietude—marked by skipping vinyl surface noise, crackling soil monitors, and smoky Fender Rhodes chords—that abruptly explodes into deafening, riot-grade wall-of-sound detonations where overblown chanter reeds and distorted 808s obliterate the mix.

Scene & Origin Lore
Phloem-Riot erupted from illegal rogue laboratories hidden in the Amazonian outskirts of Manaus, where dissident botanists collaborating with Scottish expatriate sound designers began running copper electrodes into endangered mahogany and ficus trees to track real-time illegal logging stress. Frustrated by pacifist conservation movements, they hooked these bio-transducers to MPCs, modular synth rigs, and heirloom bagpipes, throwing clandestine greenhouse raves that served as anti-deforestation combat rallies. Scene devotees, known as Grafts, dress in torn high-visibility forestry gear, chlorophyll-stained work vests, and utility harnesses interwoven with live hanging moss and coiled jumper cables. Their signature gathering ritual involves collective "sap-tapping," where MCs trade breathless, politically radical verses while passing around a shared chanter pipe rigged to the bio-voltage of an actively transpiring bromeliad.

Typical Instruments: Great Highland bagpipes (modified with internal contact pickups and digital pitch-benders), Fender Rhodes electric piano, Moog Sub 37, live botanical bio-data sonification rigs (galvanic micro-voltage sensors), heavily swung Roland TR-808 percussion, bitcrushed double bass.

